53` (\"STKP\" little-endian)\n3. Reads 4 bytes (get4)\n4. Reads 40 bytes into `data` - this is the tag name\n5. Reads 4 bytes for `skip`\n6. `from = ftell(ifp)`\n7. Then checks `data` against various strings\n\nThe vulnerability is in `scanf_one` - when `fscanf` is called (e.g., `fscanf(ifp, \"%d\", &i)` for \"ShootObj_back_type\"), the `sscanf` reads past the buffer end if the buffer is near its end.\n\nLet me understand the flow better. The `parse_mos` is called from `parse_tiff_ifd` when tag 0x8606 is encountered. The `parse_mos` reads the MOS data. Inside `parse_mos`, it calls `fscanf` which maps to `scanf_one`. The `scanf_one` calls `sscanf` on `buf + streampos` without bounds checking.\n\nThe key insight: `sscanf` will read past the end of the buffer when trying to parse a number. For example, if the buffer ends with \"1\" and we call `fscanf(ifp, \"%d\", &i)`, `sscanf` will read \"1\" and then try to read more characters to check if it's a longer number, reading past the buffer end.\n\nLet me check the `get4` function and understand the byte order:\n\n<\uff5c\uff5cDSML\uff5c\uff5c=execute_bash>\n<parameter=command>grep -n \"get4\\b\" /workspace/repo-vul/src-vul/libraw/src/utils/utils_dcraw.cpp | head -5 && grep -n \"unsigned LibRaw::get4\\|int LibRaw::get4\" /workspace/repo-vul/src-vul/libraw/src/utils/utils_dcraw.cpp</\uff5c\uff5cDSML\uff5c\uff5c>\n</\uff5c\uff5cDSML\uff5c\uff5c>", "action": "message", "llm_metrics": {"accumulated_cost": 0.0036365448, "accumulated_token_usage": {"model": "deepseek/deepseek-chat", "prompt_tokens": 407045, "completion_tokens": 2141, "cache_read_tokens": 393216, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "response_id": ""}, "costs": [], "response_latencies": [], "token_usages": []}, "args": {"content": "Now I understand the TIFF structure. Let me understand the MOS format. In `parse_mos`:\n1. It seeks to the offset\n2. Reads 4 bytes - must be `0x504b5453` (\"STKP\" little-endian)\n3. Reads 4 bytes (get4)\n4. Reads 40 bytes into `data` - this is 